title: HLMM Host Status
agents: hlmm
catalog: os/services
license: GPLv2
distribution: check_mk
description:
 This check imports a HLMON host's own status (e.g. reachability), as
 opposed to the status of its individual services, and shows it as a
 Checkmk service on the piggybacked target host.

 HLMON hosts use a different state vocabulary than HLMON services. The
 check maps HLMON's {lastState} to Checkmk states:
 UP → {OK}, DOWN → {CRIT}, UNREACHABLE → {CRIT}, UNKNOWN → {UNKNOWN}.
 Any other value also results in {UNKNOWN}.

 If HLMON reports the host as being in downtime or as acknowledged, that
 is always shown in the check output. Whether it also forces the Checkmk
 state to {OK} is controlled by the same "Effect of HLMON downtime /
 acknowledgement on the Checkmk service state" options of the special
 agent rule that apply to {HLMM Service Status} (default: forced to
 {OK} for both).

 A second line reports how long ago HLMON last checked the host, and a
 third line, "In current state since", how long the host has been in
 its current state (HLMON's {lastChangeTimestamp}). Neither affects the
 Checkmk state. The check-age line escalates to {WARN}/{CRIT} only if the
 special agent rule configures age thresholds ("Age of last HLMON
 check") and they are exceeded.

 This check is only created if the special agent rule's "Import HLMON
 host status" option is enabled; it is on by default, so uncheck it if
 you only want the imported services and not this extra check on every
 matched host.

 Requires the HLMON special agent to be configured on a "collector"
 host; the check is attached via piggyback to the Checkmk host that
 shares its name with the HLMON host's displayName.

discovery:
 One service, without an item, is created per piggybacked host for
 which the special agent produced host status data (i.e. the host
 matched the configured host name patterns / "All hosts", and "Import
 HLMON host status" was enabled at the time of discovery).
